WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
You're building

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:00.880
a new

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:01.600
search feature

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:01.920
when an

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:02.960
urgent bug needs

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:03.680
attention.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.800
Git Switch lets

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.200
you leave

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:05.760
that feature

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.400
branch and

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:06.800
work on

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:07.360
an existing

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.600
bug fix branch.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:10.400
Think of

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.040
branches as

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:11.760
different lines

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:12.960
of development inside

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.160
the same project.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:14.960
Switching makes

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.520
the bug

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.800
fix branch current,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.920
updating your working

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:18.640
files and

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.680
the staging area

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
for that target.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.860
Your saved feature

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.660
commits stay

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:23.900
on the

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.700
feature branch.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.740
Switching doesn't make

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.460
a commit.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.140
Uncommitted changes can travel

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:28.460
with you

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.580
when they're compatible,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.860
so changing branches

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:31.500
doesn't always

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.660
mean starting clean.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.080
If switching would

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.520
lose local changes,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:36.960
Git

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.840
normally stops instead.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.360
Resolve that situation

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.720
before moving on.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.000
The takeaway.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.040
Switch chooses the

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:44.680
branch you're

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:45.400
working on,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.040
so check

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.080
your context before

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:47.480
making the

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.320
next change.
